A||||10||Scott_M||Belt Tensioner Broken||||1||
Z||000000||Scott_M||08-17-2000||12:37 AM||||It seems the new battery I put into my 87 SE 4 cyl, wasn't as secure as it should have been, and moved against the serpentine belt and water pump pulley. Damage to the Battery was minimum. However, with the battery against the serpentine belt slowing it down, the belt tensioner got twisted downward, out of place. I unscrewed the bolt holding the pulley in place but it won't come off. Any ideas? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-17-2000||11:30 AM||||The tensioner must be replaced as a whole unit. there are notes about it in<A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/ogre/" TARGET=_blank> my cave</A>. Messing with the pulley likely won't help you.<P>I'm guessing you mean the tensioner isn't holding the belt tight now. Likely the spring has failed or the arm jammed. In either case the thing needs replacement. The only way the arm can jam is when the pivot bearing has failed. That will let the arm rock sideways on the pivot bolt.<P>If the arm has jammed be very careful! If it comes loose while you are working on it it will likely hurt you badly. The sping in the arm is very strong. DO NOT remove the arm pivot bolt!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Scott_M||08-17-2000||09:38 PM||||Would a belt tensioning arm from any Fiero do or do I have to replace it with one from a similiar model? I'm thinking I can go to a U-Pick it parts yard this weekend and yank one off. Not only is the tensioner not keeping the belt tight, it's just hanging loose. It rotates about 2in. in either direction but the spring is'nt working. I printed out your directions for replacing the unit.Looks like my weekend is already taken. Many Thanks!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||08-18-2000||03:33 AM||||87-88 4 cyls are the only ones that had serpentine belt tensioners. Unless you enjoy replacing tensioners, just get a new one!<P>  Now oddly enough, the 22P parts catalog shows another belt tensioner that is wrong for the 87. It is almost X shaped. The one on my 87 is more triangled shaped. My question to 88 4 cyl owners is if they have a different tensioner? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-18-2000||01:47 PM||||There is only one I know of. It is the upper alternator bracket as well as the cover to the hole in front of the block.<P>These aren't worth messing with used. The original ones had a grease in them that couldn't take the heat of being part of the water jacket. The pivot hole in the arm wears out because of it. If you can even find one used, they are only good in a pinch. I'd still plan on a new one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||BN Boomer||08-18-2000||03:40 PM||||If you need one to get by for a while, I'll give you one for the cost of shipping, $3.20. Let me know if you are interested...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Scott_M||08-18-2000||05:58 PM||||Does this thing have a part number# ? I called Pep Boys for a price check, and the guy couldn't find the part. ***Boomer thats a generous offer. I'll let you know a day or so. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-18-2000||10:44 PM||||It's a "dealer only" part. Fiero Store is the only other place I know of where you can get one.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Scott_M||08-21-2000||07:47 PM||||BN Boomer I'm real interested in taking that belt tensioner off your hands.||||reg||6
Z||000009||87ECO5||08-22-2000||07:43 AM||||Scott_M:  I had to replace my '87's back in November and, like the others stated, I had to give a Pontiac dealer about $70 for one.<P>A word of caution from someone that fought with it:  There's a small metal clip that holds wires running to the battery in place so they don't rub against the serpentine belt.  When you remove the tensioner, this clip will come with it - and good luck getting it back in place!  My clip didn't find its way back in and, so far, I've not ground through any wires!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Also, as the others said, the tensioner doubles as the cover of the water jacket.  When you remove it, about a third of your car's coolant will end up on the floor of your workspace.  So, either have a large bucket ready or, better yet, take advantage of this work and flush your coolant system at the same time.<P>Hope this helps ...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Scott_M||08-22-2000||06:47 PM||||I checked my local Pontiac dealer and they want $68, the Fiero Shop has it for $59.30, with shipping it's $66. All that for such a small piece of metal. I tell ya, I'm headed to the salvage yard. <BR>I followed Ogre's info and just drained the radiator. <BR>I was woindering what that clip was for. When I replaced my starter a few yers ago I ran a new battery wire down through there, but I never saw the clip. Subsequently, that wire got chaffed and had to be replaced. The new wire goes down the firewall opposite the engine and down to the starter. ||||reg||1
